### Slot keys and their defaults

`$slot` declares the default slot. `$slot 1` declares a positional slot
with a positive whole-number key. `$slot title` declares a named slot.
Each declaration fills its head and has no body. An occurrence may supply a
compile-time default of its receiving type.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    link #= [$html:<a href="[$slot 1 = "/"]" title="[$slot hint]">[$slot]</a>]
    guide = [link, "/guide":[$in hint:Read the guide]Guide]
]

The first loose contribution fills position 1. The named insert supplies the
hint and the remaining text fills the default slot. Each key has one receiving
type across its occurrences. A String slot joins text. A sequence slot
collects elements under its element type.

`$in` accepts one bare name. The receiving application routes it to that named
slot. Multiple inserts for the same key contribute in source order. Any routed
contribution, including an explicitly empty one, suppresses the default at
each occurrence of that key. Without a contribution, each occurrence uses its
own default or emits its type's empty result. A stored constant insert can
participate in its immediate parent's routing. Returning an unresolved insert
from a runtime function is an error. See @../templates/#template-slots (Template
slots) for routing order, repeated slots and runtime contributions.

### Compose style utilities with named inserts

A style utility can be a constant insert. Its name carries both the CSS and
the destination. Several utilities can target the same receiving wrapper.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    heading #= [$html:<h2 style="[$slot style]">[$slot]</h2>]
    heavy #= [$in style:font-weight: 700;]
    red #= [$in style:color: red;]

    warning = [heading, heavy, red:Build failed]
]

The heading receives `font-weight: 700;color: red;` in that order. To style
only part of the heading, apply an element wrapper inside its body and pass the
utility to that nested wrapper. A function that returns a String supplies CSS
text. Its caller still chooses a destination with `$in style`.

### Presence, output and evaluation

`$supplied` accepts the same default, named and positional keys as `$slot`. A
guard declares its key even if no slot prints it. It requires a colon and body,
takes no default and has no `else` form. Only a child within a template body
can act as a guard. A guard cannot occur as a statement, value, fragment root
or directive operand. Its only permitted companion in the head is one formatter.

The guard tests whether a contribution reached the key. Defaults and fallback
text do not satisfy it. A routed payload whose runtime condition emits nothing
still satisfies it. Each matching guard emits its body once, however many
contributions reached that key. An absent guard produces no output. A selected
empty body still counts as a child for `$before` and `$after`.

When a guard alone declares a key, the enclosing construction sets the receiving
type. The compiler validates the supplied payload but skips its evaluation. A
slot that also consumes the payload restores ordinary evaluation. A guard
inside supplied content tests keys in the application that authored that
content.

A stored or exported wrapper keeps its unresolved guards. A guard in a plain
loop body can survive storage, but compilation rejects a guard that would have
to cross a wrapper applied only when output exists. Examples include a
conditional child wrapper and head content before a loop in that same head.

### Entry destinations

Only a direct, top-level compile-time insert in an active entry routes to a
builder destination. Nested, stored and aliased inserts keep ordinary
template routing. The HTML builder recognises `title`, `description`, `lang`,
`favicon`, `body_style`, `stylesheets` and `head`. An entry cannot send a
runtime fragment to a named builder destination.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    [$in stylesheets:[@assets/site.css]]
    [$in head:<meta name="theme-color" content="#222222">]
]

A `stylesheets` insert contributes one tracked CSS resource. The builder
deduplicates these by resource identity. For `head`, it compares whole
rendered contributions. See @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata
and builder slots) for each destination's content and fallback rules.
